id: design.load-shedding
kind: design
title: Load shedding
aliases: load shedding, shedding, admission control, degrade
tags: reliability, overload
questions:
  - what do you do when the service is overloaded?
  - should a service reject requests when it cannot keep up?
  - your job queue grows without bound — what do you do?
invariant: Under overload, reject or downgrade work early and deliberately so the requests that are served still meet their latency budget.
answer_30s: Shed low-value work early (429 or 503 with Retry-After) instead of accepting everything and letting queues grow, because serving most requests well beats serving all of them badly.
answer_2m: |
  The reasoning is about tail latency: once utilisation passes the knee of the
  latency curve, adding load increases latency non-linearly, so accepting more
  work makes every request slower until timeouts cascade. Shedding policy,
  in order of preference: reject at the edge with a clear status and Retry-After,
  deprioritise or defer non-critical work (background refreshes, analytics,
  non-essential writes), serve degraded responses (cached, partial, stale), and
  only then queue. Shedding requires knowing the budget: an explicit concurrency
  limit or queue bound is what turns "we are overloaded" into a decision, and
  priority classes (user-facing over background) decide what to drop. It is the
  operational partner of backpressure: backpressure slows the producer,
  shedding protects the server, and a mature system uses both plus autoscaling
  for the slow-moving capacity change.
when_to_use:
  - any service with a latency objective and bursty or adversarial traffic
avoid_when:
  - dropping work that cannot be retried or reconstructed (queue or degrade instead)
minimal_example: |
  if inflight.get() >= limit:            # admission control
      return 429 with Retry-After: 1
  else:
      permit = acquire()
      try: handle()
      finally: release(permit)
failure_modes:
  - no limit at all, so overload becomes OOM or a latency collapse for everyone
  - shedding without telling the client (200 with an empty body), so callers cannot retry correctly
  - shedding the wrong class of traffic (dropping user requests while background analytics keep running)
related:
  - design.backpressure
  - design.bounded-queue
  - design.rate-limiting
  - design.timeouts
  - rust.tokio-semaphore
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/
